{"step_index":80,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:56:49Z","thinking":"**Assessing Graph Properties for Testing**\n\nHow many graphs have been tested already? Initial tests on order 20 suggest that calculating `roman_domination_number` using ILP might be time-consuming for each graph. Examining the logs is necessary to understand the testing landscape. A strategy should be designed to test graphs with maximized radius and minimal Roman domination number. The ideal candidates for this property might be paths and trees. Since all trees of order up to 14 have already been tested and have satisfied the condition, we may begin by testing other graph types.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c \\\"\\nimport networkx as nx\\nfrom graphcalc.graphs.invariants.domination import roman_domination_number\\n\\ndef test(G, name):\\n    if not nx.is_connected(G): return\\n    rad = nx.radius(G)\\n    rom = roman_domination_number(G)\\n    if rad + 1 > rom:\\n        print(f'COUNTEREXAMPLE: {name}, rad={rad}, rom={rom}, edges={list(G.edges())}')\\n        exit(0)\\n\\n# Grids\\nfor a in range(2, 6):\\n    for b in range(2, 6):\\n        test(nx.grid_2d_graph(a, b), f'grid_{a}x{b}')\\n\\n# Ladders\\nfor n in range(2, 12):\\n    test(nx.ladder_graph(n), f'ladder_{n}')\\n    test(nx.circular_ladder_graph(n), f'circular_ladder_{n}')\\n\\nprint('All structured families passed!')\\n\\\"\"","Cwd":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolAction":"\"Test structured graphs\"","toolSummary":"\"Test grids and ladders\""}}]}
