{"step_index":117,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:57:59Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:57:59+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:58:02+02:00\nThe search for \"\"rad(G) + 1 \\le \\gamma_R(G)\" OR \"rad(G) + 1 <= \\gamma_R(G)\" OR \"radius(G) + 1 \\le\"\" returned the following summary:\nThe inequality **$\\text{rad}(G) + 1 \\le \\gamma_R(G)$** (often written as $\\text{rad}(G) + 1 \\le \\gamma_R(G)$) relates the **radius** of a graph, $\\text{rad}(G)$, to its **Roman domination number**, $\\gamma_R(G)$[1][2].\n\n### Context and Explanation\n*   **$\\text{rad}(G)$ (Radius):** The minimum eccentricity of any vertex in the graph, where the eccentricity of a vertex $u$ is the maximum distance from $u$ to any other vertex in $G$[2].\n*   **$\\gamma_R(G)$ (Roman Domination Number):** The minimum weight of a Roman dominating function on $G$, where a Roman dominating function is a labeling $f: V(G) \\to \\{0, 1, 2\\}$ such that every vertex with label 0 has a neighbor with label 2[1][2].\n\n### Typical Research Context\nThis inequality is frequently explored in graph theory literature concerning bounds on domination parameters[3][4]. Research in this area often seeks to establish relationships between distance-based parameters (like radius and diameter) and domination-based parameters (like $\\gamma(G)$ or $\\gamma_R(G)$)[5][4][6].\n\n*   **Variations:** You may also find similar studies involving \"k-distance\" Roman domination numbers, where the definition is extended to consider vertices at distance at most $k$[6].\n*   **Related Bounds:** Literature often compares $\\gamma_R(G)$ with other parameters such as the domination number $\\gamma(G)$, the total domination number, and the diameter[1][5][7].\n\nIf you are looking for a specific proof or a paper that cites this inequality, it is commonly found in studies discussing the structural properties of Roman domination in general graphs or specific classes of graphs (such as trees or trapezoid graphs)[5][7][6].\n\nSources:\n[1] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHWXa1rZcUv3jpOa37LHaViNNad1FCrW-ljFHQZDQlxqWkxlwKoXKx4dJhIH3isAtSZrBhkoVa4AhzQvHo8k2rT0zL7dGO2I9Zrr1dyLoahNvaAnqf44w9vUVNC3M4gQvSg0oARU8NyL89gUrMXHbge59q5MTxbybbNz5pZk1zGKGk6LuYM0pZrdG7yfaWoQbGe)\n[2] [ejpam.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHjl-vXCXo0dWTdw5fqYJeaB5QwCfwiG2mJKCGxn1olzV8GCrWn4nV8Ug0J18HGQ4L2Ua-7mVVC4xURi9Boxc5lPgXiIPHguXdRiAj5x4ZBhyDvdiR3qJwgx8waHshCRRNGnZ5wESj8)\n[3] [scribd.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHodS4msFLFvTgKOTzIl9Sdpzk-1P_TnLar2ARWs2yqHJ8ajLFST-mtbyeK561J7WdLWkOuzVZKJVQQtCup46ReMhBn8gTUCEPgxKAX58s1yb2BOlyXoNeWCwQv6hWGzLJBC19q41wgA9lK-l0xw2M6ppsnD4d8wSsS)\n[4] [ijsrp.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGhxwgb0udl09TL2jGF25UeRfksMZ60W1ooWloYHe2RXCjaTXuz5-7M_k4FCP1ivOCjxuLZQrNN6GufWfvYIPCKmLbZ45ZejRC-3X0A0QkDdklw4JOagct9HcgU3nLsHtMOJ_MDNdMxK5BCVMKjRg==)\n[5] [ijsdr.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE3RO2yLV5My1UgiobEhwdlQ_SVRcRpilZDNE7WKgRyyuBxQjgipPt009rFvq8BKiPN_g5kuW2qUlinq4J6afIIwLPlxJTOzjWvWmoPCoxCLIGTOH_xF84OGB6WnQRvVe48IA==)\n[6] [researchgate.net](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFiIThOHPLWocQ1HYr6BJTB_tRR-VRaDsK1vOHL5QLoI_CUgkGSDpCnAV0iwSsru4uAKlSZ1HBCYQhGiJzIVI9KVBoHPsIMG-Xaj6ngoeHIXuveppRvaCrPOqJYFQTwlMgMnSnpB2G4jQhdatyYe6oYmkHw-ML20ANGuQxGxrY2OT5x6YdpiVxE-b0kBI9CnScdQveK8OzJ)\n[7] [isikun.edu.tr](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE7rHX63DhnRCVEg15KE9En_Xu4yURYORyXupm0hlsaDNzDu-Dr_HKr0cHm9L_5ko3fXsUYpXhls8maV4euNzQbnJtO0L7Bmb2H0LKXifYZ1wkYDXfUojsxddrbWLRRJaRNnLuy2N66pzTzVIZzzlOQbUSZlEsA)"}
