{"step_index":119,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:58:06Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:58:06+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:58:08+02:00\nThe search for \"site:ejpam.com \"roman domination\" radius\" returned the following summary:\nThe *European Journal of Pure and Applied Mathematics* (EJPAM) contains several articles discussing \"Roman domination\" in the context of graph theory[1][2][3]. While the term \"radius\" appears in these publications, it is typically used in two distinct ways:\n\n*   **Graph Theory Definitions:** In articles discussing Roman domination (such as *\"On The Global Distance Roman Domination of Some Graphs\"*), the **radius** of a graph $G$ is formally defined as the minimum eccentricity among all its vertices, denoted as $\\text{rad}(G) = \\min\\{\\text{ecc}(u) : u \\in V(G)\\}$[1].\n*   **Independent Research Topics:** Other articles in the journal use \"radius\" in unrelated mathematical contexts, such as *\"Radius Inequalities Involving $2 \\times 2$ Block Matrices\"*[4] or *\"Radius and Signless Laplacian Spectral Radius for Generalized Core-satellite Graphs\"*[5].\n\nIf you are looking for a specific relationship or bound involving both Roman domination and the radius of a graph, the article *\"On The Global Distance Roman Domination of Some Graphs\"* is the most relevant, as it explores distance-based variants of Roman domination[1][2][6].\n\nSources:\n[1] [ejpam.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGneooxtdatU6-UzGqt2WpdZ7yFfYjcCreqzy08gtma4l_dqyJNrmbew5ksqeeJ-5CJgvdFBKN6zyoagE1Zy7aBSd3Q-7WFJitbRdMq9HMTbacw6RCOgc3EElfG8ORo3uBT7L5KuUsT)\n[2] [ejpam.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGneooxtdatU6-UzGqt2WpdZ7yFfYjcCreqzy08gtma4l_dqyJNrmbew5ksqeeJ-5CJgvdFBKN6zyoagE1Zy7aBSd3Q-7WFJitbRdMq9HMTbacw6RCOgc3EElfG8ORo3uBT7L5KuUsT)\n[3] [ejpam.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIY