{"step_index":123,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:58:12Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:58:12+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:58:12+02:00\n{\"File\":\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/known_relations.py\",\"LineNumber\":53,\"LineContent\":\"    \\\"diameter\\\": \\\"diam\\\", \\\"radius\\\": \\\"rad\\\",\"}\n{\"File\":\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/known_relations.py\",\"LineNumber\":239,\"LineContent\":\"    \\\"independence_number \\u003e= radius\\\",\"}\n{\"File\":\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/known_relations.py\",\"LineNumber\":254,\"LineContent\":\"    \\\"independence_number \\u003c= radius\\\",\"}\n{\"File\":\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/known_relations.py\",\"LineNumber\":258,\"LineContent\":\"    \\\"diameter \\u003e= radius\\\",\"}\n{\"File\":\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/known_relations.py\",\"LineNumber\":291,\"LineContent\":\"    \\\"diameter \\u003c= 2 radius\\\",\"}\n{\"File\":\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/known_relations.py\",\"LineNumber\":292,\"LineContent\":\"    \\\"radius \\u003c= diameter\\\",\"}\n{\"File\":\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/known_relations.py\",\"LineNumber\":293,\"LineContent\":\"    \\\"radius \\u003e= 1/2 diameter\\\",\"}\n{\"File\":\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/known_relations.py\",\"LineNumber\":294,\"LineContent\":\"    \\\"radius \\u003c= independence_number\\\",\"}\n{\"File\":\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/known_relations.py\",\"LineNumber\":475,\"LineContent\":\"    \\\"independence_number \\u003c= radius\\\",\"}"}
{"step_index":124,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:58:12Z","thinking":"**Exploring Graph Property Relationships**\n\nConsidering known relationships: radius is less than or equal to the independence number, and diameter is less than or equal to twice the radius. Uncertain if radius relates to domination number, however, noted that domination number can be less than or equal to the independence number, and that radius and domination number may differ.\n\nFound a challenge where a non-trivial graph implies that radius is less than or equal to total domination number.\n\n\n**Evaluating Roman Domination Properties**\n\nConsidering the implications of the relationships:  `radius ≤ total_domination_number`, `total_domination_number ≤ roman_domination_number`, and `(radius + 1) ≤ roman_domination_number`. The relationship between total and Roman domination needs further examination. The current focus is on potentially finding graphs where the inequality `(radius + 1) ≤ roman_domination_number` does not hold.  A search strategy involves generating graphs with high radius and comparatively lower Roman domination numbers to identify a counterexample.\n\n\n**Analyzing Radius Bound in Roman Domination**\n\nConsidering the question of whether radius can ever be greater than or equal to the Roman domination number. A general proof is attempted. Defined sets based on Roman dominating function's values (0, 1, 2). Then proved all vertices are within distance 1 of V1 union V2, which forms a dominating set.\n\nThe size of this dominating set is less than or equal to the Roman domination number. Focused on whether a vertex with eccentricity at most Roman domination number minus 1 always exists. Checked this with simple cases.\n\n\n**Testing Radius vs. Roman Domination**\n\nConsidering different values of Roman domination number to determine if a vertex with eccentricity at most `Roman domination number - 1` exists. Analyzed cases where `Roman domination number` equals 3, 4, 5, and 6. For `Roman domination number` up to 6, a vertex with eccentricity less than or equal to `Roman domination number - 1` consistently ex\n<truncated 326 bytes>\nation Analysis**\n\nConsidering `Roman domination number = 8`, investigating if a similar radius bound exists. Examined various cases with different sizes for sets V1 and V2 in the Roman dominating function's definition. Focused on the case where |V2| = 4 and |V1| = 0, analyzing the potential for a high radius, constructed a path with vertices representing balls in V2 and calculated a candidate midpoint.\n\nEvaluated distances from the midpoint to various vertices, including those in the graph's neighborhoods, found the radius to be less than or equal to 5, which satisfies the inequality. Generalized the analysis by considering paths P3k, calculating their Roman domination number and radius. Tested inequality, confirming it holds for various k values.\n\n\n**Verifying Radius Bound for Paths**\n\nAnalyzed path graphs to verify the inequality `radius + 1 ≤ Roman domination number`.  Examined cases: P3k, P3k+1, P3k+2, where k is a non-negative integer. Confirmed the inequality held for all path lengths.\n\nHypothesized that paths maximize radius given a Roman domination number.  Reasoned that any other graph structure would decrease or maintain the radius.  Any shortcut edges reduce distances and, therefore, the radius.\n\n\n**Investigating Potential Counterexamples**\n\nConsidering the implications of adding edges to a path; any added edge only decreases distances. Questioning if adding edges could reduce the Roman domination number more rapidly than the radius. A Python script is being planned to search for a counterexample using simulated annealing, genetic search or exhaustive search.\n\n**Evaluating Exhaustive Search Feasibility**\n\nDetermining the feasibility of an exhaustive search for a counterexample. Noted that there are 11,117 connected graphs of order 8. Attempted to identify graph generation tools. Determined that generating all graphs of order 8 is a feasible approach.\n\n**Determining Maximum Radius**\n\nConsidering the relationship between the order of a graph and its radius. Currently focusing on the case when n = 8.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c \\\"\\nimport networkx as nx\\nfrom graphcalc.graphs.invariants.domination import roman_domination_number\\nimport itertools\\n\\n# We want to find ANY graph where radius + 1 > roman_domination_number\\n# Let's search by starting with trees of various radii, and adding edges\\nprint('Starting targeted search...')\\nfor n in range(4, 16):\\n    for T in nx.nonisomorphic_trees(n):\\n        rad_T = nx.radius(T)\\n        if rad_T < 3: continue\\n        # T has radius >= 3\\n        # Test T itself:\\n        rom_T = roman_domination_number(T)\\n        if rad_T + 1 > rom_T:\\n            print('Found tree counterexample!')\\n            exit(0)\\n        # Try adding 1, 2, 3 edges to T that don't decrease radius\\n        non_edges = list(nx.non_edges(T))\\n        for e in non_edges:\\n            G = T.copy()\\n            G.add_edge(*e)\\n            if nx.radius(G) == rad_T:\\n                rom_G = roman_domination_number(G)\\n                if rad_T + 1 > rom_G:\\n                    print(f'Counterexample found with 1 added edge! n={n}')\\n                    exit(0)\\nprint('Targeted search passed!')\\n\\\"\"","Cwd":"\"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"10000","toolAction":"\"Targeted edge addition search\"","toolSummary":"\"Search graphs by adding edges to trees\""}}],"truncated_fields":["thinking"]}
{"step_index":125,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"RUNNING","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:58:28Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:58:28+02:00\nTool is running as a background task with task id: dcc102cf-facd-4d9b-8d91-c684bfca7058/task-125\nTask Description: /mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport networkx as nx\nfrom graphcalc.graphs.invariants.domination import roman_domination_number\nimport itertools\n\n# We want to find ANY graph where radius + 1 > roman_domination_number\n# Let's search by starting with trees of various radii, and adding edges\nprint('Starting targeted search...')\nfor n in range(4, 16):\n    for T in nx.nonisomorphic_trees(n):\n        rad_T = nx.radius(T)\n        if rad_T < 3: continue\n        # T has radius >= 3\n        # Test T itself:\n        rom_T = roman_domination_number(T)\n        if rad_T + 1 > rom_T:\n            print('Found tree counterexample!')\n            exit(0)\n        # Try adding 1, 2, 3 edges to T that don't decrease radius\n        non_edges = list(nx.non_edges(T))\n        for e in non_edges:\n            G = T.copy()\n            G.add_edge(*e)\n            if nx.radius(G) == rad_T:\n                rom_G = roman_domination_number(G)\n                if rad_T + 1 > rom_G:\n                    print(f'Counterexample found with 1 added edge! n={n}')\n                    exit(0)\nprint('Targeted search passed!')\n\"\nTask logs are available at: file:///tmp/agyhome.NWyWnj/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/dcc102cf-facd-4d9b-8d91-c684bfca7058/.system_generated/tasks/task-125.log\nYOU MUST TAKE ONE OF THE FOLLOWING TWO ACTIONS: A) either proceed to other relevant work (if any) or, B) simply update the user with a short message (that you have launched the command and will wait for it to finish) and end the turn.\n DO NOTHING ELSE."}
