{"step_index":148,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:59:38Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:59:38+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:59:38+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9/reference/GraphInvariantsComputable.lean`\nTotal Lines: 537\nTotal Bytes: 27292\nShowing lines 1 to 60\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: /-\n2: GraphInvariantsComputable.lean — a *computable* mirror of the invariants in\n3: `GraphInvariants.lean`, so that a claim about one explicit finite graph can be\n4: settled by `decide` rather than by a hand proof.\n5: \n6: Why this file exists\n7: --------------------\n8: `GraphInvariants.lean` defines the invariants the way a mathematician states\n9: them: `dominationNumber G = sInf { n | ∃ s, s.card = n ∧ G.IsDominatingSet s }`.\n10: That is the right *specification*, but `sInf` over a `Set ℕ` (under\n11: `open scoped Classical`) is **noncomputable**, so the kernel cannot evaluate it\n12: on a concrete graph — measured directly, `decide` succeeds on `order`, `size`\n13: and `minDegree`, and fails on every `sInf`/`sSup` invariant (domination,\n14: annihilation, the zero-forcing family, and mathlib's own `indepNum`/`cliqueNum`).\n15: \n16: That blocks the cheapest source of formal results we have. The refutation engine\n17: kills thousands of candidates and knows exactly which graph kills each one; each\n18: such pair is a `¬∀ …` theorem whose proof is \"evaluate both sides on this graph\"\n19: — but only if the invariants actually evaluate.\n20: \n21: The definitions here are the same mathematical objects expressed as a minimum\n22: over an explicitly enumerated `Finset`, which the kernel *can* reduce. They are\n23: deliberately kept in their own namespace: nothing in the existing proof pipeline\n24: changes, and `GraphInvariants.lean` remains the specification of record.\n25: -/\n26: import Mathlib\n27: \n28: open Finset\n29: \n30: namespace GraphCalc\n31: \n32: variable {V : Type*} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n33: \n34: /-- Least cardinality of a subset satisfying `p`, or `0` when none does.\n35: \n36: The `0` fallback is only harmless when *some* subset satisfies `p` — for\n37: `indepNum`, `dominationNumber` and `vertexCoverNumber`, `univ` always does. It\n38: is NOT harmless in general, and treating it as such produced a real defect:\n39: `IsConnectedZeroForcingSet` is unsatisfiable on a disconnected graph (`univ`\n40: does not induce a connected subgraph), so the invariant silently read `0` where\n41: graphcalc reports \"undefined\". A conjecture `Z_c + 2 ≤ 2 * Z_t` then became\n42: `2 ≤ 0` and was refutable with no mathematical content.\n43: \n44: Any new invariant built on `minCard` must either prove `univ` satisfies its\n45: predicate, or expose a `Has…` witness predicate below and be exported under it. -/\n46: def minCard (p : Finset V → Prop) [DecidablePred p] : ℕ :=\n47:   ((univ.powerset.filter p).image Finset.card).min.getD 0\n48: \n49: /-- Some subset satisfies `p`: exactly the precondition `minCard` needs for its\n50: value to mean \"the least cardinality\" rather than \"there were none\". -/\n51: def HasWitness (p : Finset V → Prop) [DecidablePred p] : Prop :=\n52:   ∃ s ∈ (univ : Finset V).powerset, p s\n53: \n54: instance (p : Finset V → Prop) [DecidablePred p] : Decidable (HasWitness p) := by\n55:   unfold HasWitness; infer_instance\n56: \n57: /-- Greatest `k ≤ n` satisfying `p`, or `0` when none does. -/\n58: def maxUpTo (n : ℕ) (p : ℕ → Prop) [DecidablePred p] : ℕ :=\n59:   (((range (n + 1)).filter p).max).getD 0\n60: \nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
